# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mobile environment configuration
#
# Copy this file to `.env` inside mobile/:
#     Windows PowerShell : Copy-Item .env.example .env
#     macOS / Linux      : cp .env.example .env
#
# SECURITY - read before adding anything here.
#
# Expo inlines every EXPO_PUBLIC_-prefixed variable into the JavaScript bundle
# at build time. Anything in this file is readable by anyone who unpacks the
# app. It is public configuration, not configuration that merely happens to be
# stored on the client.
#
# NEVER put a secret here: no API keys, no database credentials, no tokens,
# no Django SECRET_KEY. Secrets belong in the root .env, which only the
# backend reads and which is never bundled.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Base URL of the Django REST API, including the version prefix and a trailing
# slash. This is the ONLY place the backend URL is configured; no screen
# hardcodes it (src/config/env.ts is the single reader).
#
# The /api/v1/ suffix is part of the value. Services ask for paths relative to
# it ('extraction/', 'compliance/'), so an origin on its own would send every
# request to the wrong place.
#
# A phone is not the computer running Django, so "localhost" does not work on
# a device. Leave this UNSET in development and the app derives the address
# from the machine serving Metro (the one you ran `npx expo start` on), on
# port 8000. Set it explicitly only when the backend is elsewhere:
#
#   Physical phone, backend on your laptop's LAN address:
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.20:8000/api/v1/
#   Android emulator, backend on the same machine (10.0.2.2 is the host):
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://10.0.2.2:8000/api/v1/
#   iOS simulator, backend on the same Mac:
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/api/v1/
#
# The backend must be started on all interfaces for a device to reach it:
#     python manage.py runserver 0.0.0.0:8000
# and your LAN address must be in DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS in the root .env.
#EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Production
#
# `npx expo start` reads this file (via your .env copy). A production build
# (`npx expo export`, `eas build`) does not need it: Expo loads
# `mobile/.env.production`, which is committed and points at the deployed
# Railway API:
#
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://legalmetrology-compliance-production.up.railway.app/api/v1/
#
# The value is baked into the bundle at build time, so changing it means
# rebuilding. A shell variable overrides both files:
#
#     EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL="https://other-host/api/v1/" npx expo export
#
# Production traffic is HTTPS only: a release build on Android refuses
# cleartext (http://) connections, and iOS App Transport Security does the same.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
